  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA departing cold front early this morning will allow for high \npressure to build southeast across the area today. A few light \nshowers are possible across far northeast Ohio and northwest \nPennsylvania early this morning before dry conditions and near-\nnormal temperatures return today. Highs today will be a bit cooler \nthan recent days with highs in the upper 50s to low 60s with mostly \nsunny skies. On Saturday, highs will be in the low 60s with \nincreasing cloud cover expected. Overnight lows tonight will be the \ncoldest in northwest Pennsylvania with temperatures falling into the \nlow to mid 30s, but elsewhere will remain in the 40s. Lows on \nSaturday night will be milder, falling into the mid to upper 40s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAs the aforementioned high pressure drifts towards the Mid-Atlantic \nCoast on Sunday, a weak warm front will lift north across the area \nbringing the potential for isolated, light rain showers. The \nassociated low pressure and upper level trough are expected to \nslowly build east into the region through midweek next week, \nincreasing the potential for showers mid to late week. Overall \nconfidence in the best timing for showers is low given model \ndivergence in the progression of the system as the dominant ridging \npattern recently observed slowly becomes progressive. Temperatures \nthrough the period will continue to remain above normal with high \npeaking on Tuesday and Wednesday when temperatures climb into the low \nto mid 70s.",
